---
title: "stdlib-cbor"
author: bad-antics
date: 2026-04-05
abstract: Core encode/decode for CBOR (RFC 8949). Covers major types (uint, nint, bytes, str, array, map, tag, simple), argument encoding, and canonical vectors from RFC 8949 Appendix A.

# Major Types

CBOR's initial byte splits into a 3-bit major type and a 5-bit argument. The module exposes constants for all eight major types.

// Major type constants
mt_uint()    // 0
mt_nint()    // 1
mt_bytes()   // 2
mt_str()     // 3
mt_arr()     // 4
mt_map()     // 5
mt_tag()     // 6
mt_simple()  // 7

# Encoding

encode(v) serializes a value into a list of byte integers following RFC 8949. Unsigned integers, negative integers, byte strings, text strings, arrays, maps, and simple values (null, true, false, floats) are supported.

// Encode an unsigned integer
encode(24)   // [24]

// Encode a small negative integer (-1)
encode(-1)   // [32]

// Encode a text string
encode("a")  // [97, 97]

# Decoding

decode(data) parses a CBOR byte list back into its original value, returning the value and the number of bytes consumed.

// Decode [24] -> 24
decode([24])  // (24, 1)

// Decode [32] -> -1
decode([32])  // (-1, 1)

# Canonical Vectors (RFC 8949 Appendix A)

Fixed test vectors used for interoperability:

- 0 -> [0]
- 1 -> [1]
- 23 -> [23]
- 24 -> [24, 24]
- 255 -> [25, 0, 255]
- -1 -> [32]
- -24 -> [55]
- true -> [245]
- false -> [244]
- null -> [246]
- "" -> [96]
- "a" -> [97, 97]
- [] -> [128]
- [1, 2, 3] -> [131, 1, 2, 3]
- {} -> [160]

# Executable Program

fn main() {
    print(encode(24))
    print(decode([24]))
    print(encode("a"))
    print(decode([97, 97]))
}

  </script>
  <script src="../ltlml.js"></script>
